{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a distributed unique ID generator?

A distributed unique ID generator gives each new order, message, upload, or event an identifier that no other generator can issue. It matters because a database sequence is simple but can become a shared dependency, while random strings often cost more storage and do not sort naturally.

In plain language, a Snowflake-style generator makes an ID from three coordinates: **when** it was created, **which generator** created it, and **which request** it was within that millisecond. Its invariant is precise: no two live generators may emit the same timestamp, generator identity, and sequence tuple.

This case study designs a positive 64-bit, roughly time-sortable ID for a multi-region product. You will turn requirements into a bit budget, make the request path independent, then test the clock and identity failures that can still break uniqueness.
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the ID contract before choosing fields" %}

## Define what an ID promises

An ID is not a database row and it is not automatically a security token. Start by deciding what callers may rely on, because that determines whether a local, sortable integer is the right tool.

### Functional requirements

- Issue one unique numeric ID for every accepted create request.
- Keep IDs roughly ordered by creation time so common index and range-scan patterns remain efficient.
- Generate locally with no database or consensus call on the normal request path.
- Decode an ID during investigation to recover its timestamp, generator identity, and sequence position.

### Non-functional constraints

- **Scale:** 1,000 active generators, each able to absorb a burst of 50,000 IDs per second.
- **Latency:** in-process generation normally completes well below 1 ms.
- **Lifetime:** retain a format that lasts at least 30 years from a chosen custom epoch.
- **Availability:** a bad generator may fail closed; it must never preserve availability by risking a duplicate.

{% callout variant="info" %}
"Roughly ordered" means IDs from healthy clocks usually increase with time. It does not mean a globally serial order across regions, nor does it establish the business order of two concurrent writes.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="capacity" title="2. Turn the traffic target into a bit budget" %}

## Spend bits only after doing the math

With a 12-bit sequence, one generator can issue `2^12 = 4,096` IDs in a millisecond, or about 4.096 million per second. That is far above a 50,000 IDs/s target. Ten generator bits support `2^10 = 1,024` active identities. The remaining 41 data bits for milliseconds last about 69.7 years from the custom epoch.

Use the lab to choose a format. It calculates the lifetime, number of generator identities, and burst capacity from the same 63 usable bits. A design is only viable when it clears all three requirements; adding machine bits always removes capacity from time or sequence.

{% interactive-block id="case-studies/unique-id-generator-bit-budget-lab" /%}

### Planning estimates

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="50M/s" label="Fleet target" detail="1,000 generators at a 50K IDs/s burst each." tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="4.096M/s" label="Per-generator ceiling" detail="12 sequence bits across 1,000 milliseconds." tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="69.7 years" label="Time range" detail="41 milliseconds bits after a custom epoch." tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="1,024" label="Live identities" detail="10 generator bits, with no duplicate lease." tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The common 1/41/10/12 allocation is a useful starting point, not a standard. A fleet with fewer generator identities might buy a longer lifetime; a very bursty generator might need more sequence bits or deliberate load spreading.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Keep the generation path local and the control plane strict" %}

## Separate issuing an ID from authorizing a generator

The hot path reads a monotonic millisecond value, updates a small in-memory sequence, and packs fields into a signed 64-bit integer. It should not wait for a network service. A control plane still assigns identities and establishes whether a process is allowed to use one.

{% system-flow title="Normal ID generation path" caption="The request path stays local. The lease and time-health checks are established before a generator accepts traffic." %}
{% system-node title="Acquire identity lease" eyebrow="Control plane" icon="key-round" tone="violet" %}
Give one generator identity to one live process, with renewal and fencing before a replacement starts.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Read safe millisecond" eyebrow="Local guard" icon="clock-3" tone="blue" %}
Reject a timestamp below the process's last emitted timestamp; do not silently move backwards.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Advance sequence" eyebrow="In memory" icon="list-ordered" tone="cyan" %}
Increment within the millisecond; wait for the next one if the finite sequence range is exhausted.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Pack and return" eyebrow="No network call" icon="binary" tone="green" %}
Shift timestamp, generator ID, and sequence into one positive integer, then return it to the caller.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### The packed form

`id = ((timestamp - epoch) << 22) | (generatorId << 12) | sequence`

The shifts are not arbitrary: 22 is the sum of 10 generator bits and 12 sequence bits. Keeping time in the most significant field makes IDs from later milliseconds numerically larger when clocks behave.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="storage" title="4. Store the minimum durable state that preserves the invariant" %}

## The generator needs little data, but its leases need evidence

The normal generator state is tiny: the last timestamp and sequence are in memory. The durable control-plane record is more important than the request-path storage because it prevents two live processes from sharing a generator identity after a restart, failover, or autoscaling event.

| Record | Owner | Why it exists | Correctness rule |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Generator lease | Identity registry | Binds a generator ID to a process, zone, and expiry | One unexpired holder per identity |
| Fencing generation | Lease service | Makes newer ownership distinguishable from stale ownership | A stale process cannot renew or be routed traffic |
| Time-health record | Generator telemetry | Reports offset, rollback events, and last safe timestamp | An unsafe clock removes the process from service |
| ID audit sample | Observability store | Decodes selected IDs for incident analysis | Sampling must never become a synchronous dependency |

Do not persist the sequence counter for every ID. That would turn a local operation into a write bottleneck. Instead, fail a generator closed on restart or clock uncertainty until it has a valid identity lease and safe time source.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="failure-lab" title="5. Trace clock, sequence, and identity failures" %}

## A local algorithm still has distributed failure modes

Choose a scenario, then select a node in the map to inspect who owns the response. The active path changes with the failure: a safe system removes only the unsafe generator or waits for the next millisecond, rather than issuing a potentially duplicate ID.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/unique-id-generator/data/clock-and-identity-topology.json" /%}

### Required failure behavior

- **Clock rollback:** stop the affected generator, quarantine it from routing, and wait until the wall clock catches the last emitted timestamp or switch to an explicitly safe logical-time policy.
- **Sequence exhaustion:** wait for the next millisecond after all sequence values are used; never wrap the counter inside the same timestamp.
- **Duplicate identity lease:** fence and drain the stale process before a replacement becomes eligible. A load balancer health check alone is not an ownership protocol.
- **Epoch exhaustion:** alert years before the timestamp field rolls over, then migrate clients to a new versioned format before the old epoch ends.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="reliability" title="6. Scale by adding isolated generators, not a central counter" %}

## Make degradation explicit

Capacity scales nearly linearly with healthy generators because each owns a disjoint generator ID. A deployment should route away from a generator whose lease, clock, or sequence guard fails; the remaining fleet continues issuing IDs within its own ranges.

### Reliability plan

- Run generators in multiple zones and assign leases from a highly available control plane.
- Keep generator utilization below a measured threshold so one zone's loss does not force all survivors into sequence-exhaustion waits.
- Use time synchronization and alert on offset, leap handling, rollback events, and long waits for the next millisecond.
- Expose generation rejects, lease conflicts, sequence waits, IDs per generator, and decoded timestamp skew as first-class metrics.
- Load-test recovery by killing a leased process, starting a replacement, and proving that the old process cannot issue IDs after fencing.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not retry indefinitely against an unhealthy local generator. Retrying preserves latency pressure but cannot repair a duplicate-risking clock or identity condition; route to another safe generator instead.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="7. Compare formats against the contract, not fashion" %}

## Every ID format makes a different promise

Choose the representation from the property that is hardest to recover later. A random UUID is excellent when independent creation matters most; a database sequence is excellent when one database already owns the write order; a Snowflake-style integer is strong when compactness, locality, and rough time order all matter.

| Approach | Strongest property | Cost | Use it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| UUIDv4 | No coordination for uniqueness | 128 bits, random index locality, no embedded time | IDs are created independently and ordering is irrelevant |
| Database sequence | Simple total order at one writer | Shared availability and scaling boundary | A single database is already the authoritative write path |
| UUIDv7 | Time-friendly standard UUID shape | 128 bits and implementation-specific sequencing details | Interoperability matters more than compact integers |
| Snowflake-style 64-bit | Compact local generation and rough time order | Clock and identity control plane | Many writers need sortable numeric IDs at low latency |

The key trade-off is not "coordination versus no coordination." Snowflake removes coordination from each ID request, then moves the critical coordination to identity leases, routing, time health, and incident response.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrap-up" title="8. Ship with an operational contract and a migration plan" %}

## Treat the format as a long-lived API

Once IDs appear in URLs, logs, databases, and client caches, changing their fields is a compatibility project. Document the epoch, bit layout, decoder, ownership protocol, and rollback behavior before the first production client depends on them.

### Launch checklist

1. Prove the chosen allocation clears current and projected lifetime, generator-count, and burst requirements.
2. Test duplicate-lease, stale-process, rollback, sequence-exhaustion, and zone-loss scenarios in an environment that resembles production routing.
3. Publish a decoder and a support runbook that explains whether an ID's timestamp is creation time, acceptance time, or only an ordering hint.
4. Set alerts for lease conflict, clock skew, reject rate, sequence wait, and date-of-epoch-exhaustion.
5. Reserve a versioning or migration strategy before the timestamp horizon becomes an emergency.

The durable lesson is that the 64-bit formula is the easy part. Global uniqueness comes from protecting the tuple around it: one live owner for each generator identity, a non-regressing timestamp, and a sequence that never wraps inside that timestamp.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

